Looking at this problem, I need to find $\lim_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n(x) - f(x)|$ given that $f_n \to f$ a.e., $\int_E |f| = a$, and $\int_E |f_n| \to b$.

**Answer Leak Check:** The problem statement contains no leaked solutions, proof sketches, or answer values. Proceeding to solve.

---

## Proof

**Setup and preliminary observation.** Since $f_n \to f$ a.e. on $E$, we have $|f_n| \to |f|$ a.e. and $|f_n - f| \to 0$ a.e. By Fatou's lemma applied to $\{|f_n|\}$:
$$\int_E |f| \le \liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n| = b,$$
so $a \le b$, confirming $b - a \ge 0$.

**Lower bound.** By the triangle inequality, $|f_n| \le |f| + |f_n - f|$, hence
$$\int_E |f_n - f| \ge \int_E |f_n| - \int_E |f| = \int_E |f_n| - a.$$
Taking $\liminf$ of both sides:
$$\liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n - f| \ge b - a. \tag{1}$$

**Upper bound via Fatou.** Define
$$g_n := \bigl(|f_n| + |f|\bigr) - |f_n - f|.$$
By the triangle inequality, $|f_n - f| \le |f_n| + |f|$, so $g_n \ge 0$ on $E$. Since $f_n \to f$ a.e., we have $|f_n| \to |f|$ a.e. and $|f_n - f| \to 0$ a.e., so
$$g_n \to \bigl(|f| + |f|\bigr) - 0 = 2|f| \quad \text{a.e.}$$
Applying Fatou's lemma to the non-negative sequence $\{g_n\}$:
$$\int_E 2|f| \le \liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_E g_n = \liminf_{n\to\infty}\left(\int_E |f_n| + \int_E |f| - \int_E |f_n - f|\right).$$
Since $\int_E |f_n| \to b$ and $\int_E |f| = a$, the expression inside the $\liminf$ has its first two terms converging to $b + a$, so:
$$2a \le b + a - \limsup_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n - f|.$$
Rearranging:
$$\limsup_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n - f| \le b - a. \tag{2}$$

**Conclusion.** Combining (1) and (2):
$$b - a \le \liminf_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n - f| \le \limsup_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n - f| \le b - a.$$
Therefore the limit exists and
$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty} \int_E |f_n(x) - f(x)| = b - a.}$$

